[2025-04-14 22:05:01,009][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-0 << "                                    &lt;span&gt;[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:01,009][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-0 << "                                        &lt;strong&gt;Compromising Threat Actor Communications (12 minute read)&lt;/strong&gt;[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:01,009][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-0 << "                                    &lt;/span&gt;[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:01,009][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-0 << "&lt;/a&gt;[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:01,009][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-0 << "&lt;br&gt;[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:01,009][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-0 << "&lt;br&gt;[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:01,009][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-0 << "&lt;span style=&quot;font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif;&quot;&gt;[\n]"
[2025-04-14 22:05:01,009][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-0 << "                                    Many threat actors utilize Telegram bots as a method of establishing C2 communication. In this technique, a threat actor creates a new Telegram bot, embeds a token for the bot in their malware and deploys it, then malware then periodically polls Telegram&apos;s servers to check for new commands sent to the bot and executes any commands that are sent while relaying the results back to the Telegram server. This post deep dives into a case study in which a malware that was discovered on VirusTotal was hijacked by the author, who discovered data from the malware developer&apos;s system which they used for testing.[\n]"
