[2025-04-14 22:05:03,898][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-2 << "ot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-SDvgyu6m58HhCZ9db8DGJbvSJKD9WLSjsoLgyGGx4hawOzuxwj806VGqb7dgIj-2667wL9EphullDX70OgkXqZAh-4UYnXrmi1bQS6d6fRnCU9hd2vXF1-LxpRlKr24gTB9akh9GciU181NXctxLyGkqpWcJ9oF8Cp-5leIXcUxOMNEghebD3DyLe0baBS8KGey4_sF-IiQRaLr5zZW63N757ksG51k61hKWWqr6fvK4pqh2mlyF-jmLv6TBw0kINQIvMCp9Ny5qf6VDTu72I/4er/A_O0QUTdQvKpB6LLxIdwkg/h31/h001.hYP7llsxHGVY4xSVbUsCk-W0IWkKnJg-RWJaA5C-xzU&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Jay Jacobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, sharing results from 11.4 million SCA scans, 12.2 million SCA findings, GitHub metadata, and OpenSSF scorecards from its weekly scan of the 1M most critical OSS projects. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style=&quot;padding-bottom:12px;padding-left:37px;padding-right:27px;padding-top:12px;&quot; class=&quot;ee&quot;&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;margin-left:0px;&quot; class=&quot;edm_outlooklist&quot;&gt;&lt;ul style=&quot;list-style-type:disc;margin:0px 0px;padding:0px 0px 0px 0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt; When reviewing libraries actually being used in (mostly) Enterprise-grade applications, only about half of them had an OpenSSF score. 96% of the libraries scored by OpenSSF are not seen in Veracode[0xe2][0x80][0x99]s dataset. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt; They point out some ways in which OpenSSF scores are limited by tooling, such as it may miss detecting certain packaging workflows, and only recognizes 3 SAST tools. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt; They threw all the OpenSSF factors, Veracode data set, and GitHub metadata into a model using an elastic net to determine the variables that are strong contributors to having open source vulnerabilities. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt; [0xe2][0x80][0x9c]79% of the time, developers never update third-party libraries once introducing them to a codebase.[0xe2][0x80][0x9d] [0xf0][0x9f][0x98][0x85][0xc2][0xa0] &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li class=&quot;listItem ultext&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;padding:0px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt; Note: Don[0xe2][0x80][0x99]t use the OpenSSF[0xe2][0x80][0x99]s BigQuery public dataset of their weekly scorecard results, use the Scorecard API instead, much more complete data. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt; [0xf0][0x9f][0x92][0xa1]Note to Future Clint: Play with elastic nets when you want to weigh the importance of a bunch of variables. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;&lt;table role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;100%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot; style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=&quot;transparent&quot; style=&quot;background-color:transparent;padding:0.0px 0.0px 0.0px 0.0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;table role=&quot;none&quot; width=&quot;100%&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;0&quot;&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;lef